1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A factorisation is a one-time cost that pays for every later solve. LU splits A into a lower-triangular L and an upper-triangular U, with A equal to L times U.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
At n = 10 the factorisation costs 705 multiplications and divisions. Each extra right-hand side then costs only n(n-1) + n squared = 190 more.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So for a single right-hand side, plain elimination at 705 wins. From two right-hand sides on, the LU route wins: 1085 against 1410.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Read the product entry by entry: entry (i,j) of LU is the dot product of row i of L with column j of U.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,000
L has n(n-1)/2 unknown off-diagonal entries and U has n(n+1)/2, which together is exactly n squared — the number of entries in A.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
That leaves one free choice, and the convention is that the diagonal of L equals one. With that, the pair is unique whenever it exists.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Doolittle is Gaussian elimination with the multipliers kept. Step one uses the first pivot, a11 = 2, to clear the two entries below it.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Each multiplier is entry divided by pivot: m21 = -4/2 = -2 and m31 = 2/2 = 1. Those two numbers are stored, not discarded.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Row one of A passes into U unchanged, the multipliers pass into column one of L, and the trailing block is updated.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Step two works on the trailing block alone: after step one it is the two-by-two block with entries 1, 1, 1, 3, so the pivot is now 1.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The multiplier m32 = 1/1 = 1 clears the last entry, and u33 = 3 - 1 times 1 = 2. The matrices are complete.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Multiply L by U back: all nine entries return, so this is a factorisation and not just bookkeeping.

13
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Solving Ax = b with the factors is two triangular solves. First Ly = b by forward substitution, which needs no division because L is unit lower triangular.

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
y = (4, 3, 0.5): each entry is b_i minus the terms already found. Then Ux = y by back substitution, which does divide.

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
x = (0, 2, -1). GATE CSE 2022 asked for L32, U33 and x1 together, and the answers are -1/2, -1/2 and 0.

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The factors carry the determinant for free: det(A) = det(L) times det(U), and det(L) = 1 because its diagonal is all ones.

17
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
On this 4x4 that is 4 times 7/4 times 12/7 times 5/3 = 20, which the cofactor expansion confirms independently.

18
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Each column of the inverse is one more pair of triangular solves, so the inverse costs n times what one solve costs.

19
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:10,000
A factorisation can fail even when A is perfectly invertible. This matrix has determinant -1, so it is invertible.

20
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:20,000
But its second leading principal minor is 1 times 1 minus 1 times 1 = 0, and elimination reaches a zero pivot at step two. Division by zero, no LU.

21
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The theorem needs its assumption: LU without row swaps exists and is unique exactly when every leading principal minor up to n-1 is nonzero. Swapping rows restores it as PA = LU.

22
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Which diagonal holds the ones is a convention, not part of the mathematics. Ones on L is Doolittle; ones on U is Crout.

23
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:50,000
GATE CSE 2015 Set 1 fixed the diagonal of U to ones on this matrix and asked for l22 of L: that is 5, not the 1 that Doolittle would give.

24
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Same A, a different pair of matrices, the same product. In general A = L D L transpose, and for a positive definite matrix the square-root form is the Cholesky factor.

25
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:10,000
The cost is measured here rather than quoted: the algorithm is instrumented, and its multiplications, divisions and subtractions are counted for n = 2 to 12.

26
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Every count matches (2/3)n cubed + (1/2)n squared - (7/6)n exactly: 5, 19, 46, 90, 155, 245, 364, 516, 705, 935, 1210.

27
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:30,000
The leading term is (2/3)n cubed, the same as elimination. LU is not cheaper per solve; it is cheaper per additional right-hand side.

28
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:40,000
GATE CSE 2025 Set 2 asked which statements about P = LU are true. Statement A: solve Ly = Q and then Ux = y — true, that is what the factors are for.

29
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
B: if P is invertible then L and U are invertible — true, since the determinant is one times the product of the pivots. C: if P is singular then some diagonal of U is zero — true, by the same product.

30
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
D: if P is symmetric then L and U are symmetric — false. This symmetric P gives L = [[1,0],[1/2,1]] and U = [[4,2],[0,2]], and neither is symmetric.

